sion assay  and and what caught our eye on this  assay was the amount of  nucleic acid input that is required for  this assay  as well as the turnaround time and and  as well as the sample types that can be  used both the ffp  and circulating tumor dna  secondary considerations obviously you  don't buy an instrument for just one  test  is what can we add to the our menu uh in  addition to the alkaline precision assay  and that would be  uh tcr beta as well as a comprehensive  and into that the myeloid assay so those  are all considerations that we had  once for future uh growth on the  instrumentation  so first we'll look at a little bit on  operational efficiency  i think uh just for comparison's sake i  have  um some things on what we we've been  doing here at cedar sinai  um over the past six years and we  started out with  a 50 gene cancer hotspot panel  using the pgm platform  back when we first started it was about  14 hours of technologists hands-on time  over two and a half days a significant  amount of time  to be used to do the test but also the  challenge of having multiple  technologists do this test and um  getting the consistency that you would  be interested in getting  on on an assay in 2016  we moved to the 50 gene cancer hot spot  panel  using the ion chef and the s5 sequencer  that was a significant change for us  in that we were able to now automate  some of the processes  and allow free up a technologist to  um to be able to other testing  and reduce the labor on the test so at  this point it was about  three hours of technologies hands-on  time over two and a half days  and that was the limiting factors back  then what were the  number of um the number of chefs  and the sequencing time was a little bit  longer in 2018 we brought on the  alkamine comprehensive assay  using iron chef and s5 sequencers  and this is about the same three and a  half hours of time on  hands-on time over about a day and a day  and a quarter  about 30 hours and the game changer here  was that we were able to acquire  five ion shafts and two s5 primes  to be able to uh perform and optimize  the workflow  for for the assay create being able to  create  multiple libraries at the same time as  well as templating  so this was uh this is pretty much the  most efficient that we've been  and so when we started to look at the  genexis system we say well what  what would that change and what's going  to change and if we can reduce those  that time with the three hours to you  know a third of that  or an hour or less than an hour that is  that's  you know that's efficiency that we would  look at  um also the the assay completion time  was significantly less than our current  assay  in 16 to 21 hours and i think  part of in the in the laboratory world  i believe that you know scheduling is  the most challenging part of  performing test testing in that you have  to be able to  uh schedule the people and they also  have to have days off so having a  flexible schedule  is one of the more important things from  the operational efficiency standpoint  okay i think i'll talk a little bit  about cost analysis  or what goes into the cost evaluation  when you get an instrument and relative  to the genexis  we wanted to uh take a look at  what is single gene testing versus  the oncoming precision assay and  yet the single gene testing paradigm has  been in place for  many many years and the need for those  single genes  is very important and that has been the  standard within  within the laboratory but and the reason  for that standard is that the  there is a need for fast turnaround time  for those  genes of interest uh in able to  address this in performing the tests we  think that the  alkali precision assay on the genexis  may be that that solution  and that it can perform testing on a  single gene  and allow us to not have to  run multiple runs of of single gene  tests per week  single gene testing also has had a  significant  impact on the technology's time and cost  for the individual  assays in addition to the higher amount  of nucleic acid that is required to do  single multiple single gene tests  uh moving on to an ngs platform  and the alkaline precision assay this  will allow  for evaluation of multiple genes  simultaneously  and this is the positive aspect of doing  you know doing sequencing  a turnaround time does vary depending on  the size of the platform the testing  that's being being done as well as  bioinformatics and the analysis portion  of the assay so by consolidating  the gene content it does reduce the cost  per gene relative to doing individual  gene testing  relative to the oncoming precision assay  we  feel that it will show a reduction in  labor  and turnaround time to on the order of  single gene testing  with an output of multiple genes  so a little bit on bioinformatics and  support  bioinformatics is an area that is  there is an increasing focus on  bioinformatics  has become you know as important if not  more important than the wet  bench processes in sequencing  and with bioinformatic pipelines there's  a huge variation  in how they are constructed whether  they're built  on a standard platform or built in-house  and what variants are called what  annotations are done  and what reports are generated  the appeal for the genexis system is  that it has a  consolidated solution for a lot while  also allowing  access of the raw data for evaluation  of you know what the calls are and what  calls are made versus what calls that  are not made  relative to support support when you  purchase  an assay or an instrument is probably  the most key component  it is important for the success of  continued operation to have  support from both the vendor  as well as on the assay  itself so the advantages to having a  unique vendor would be  we were able to optimize the assay on  the platform  with the assistance of the vendor also  from the perspective  of troubleshooting when there are  problems with the assay  if you are using if you're not using  this compatible vendor or the same  vendor  you often run into difficulties in  troubleshooting  your assay because it's hard to  determine whether it is the acid  performance or it's the instrument  performance and i think  the most important part is also from the  from the support  perspective is what type of service or  repair do you get  from that vendor and by having all of  these things consolidated on one  platform both the assay and the  instrument  it gives us the ability to be able to  call and both  troubleshoot and if it is a service  related thing have somebody come out and  fix that the instrument as well  so with the genexis  what impact would it have on the  laboratory in the molecular pathology  lab  and so what we we're seeing is that  we're hoping to use this  to expand our offerings with a  smaller sequencing panel this would be  looked at as a supplement to a larger  panel  and as we all know that the panels are  increasing  in the number of genes but along with  that we also know that  it does increase the turnaround time  when you increase the genes so  having the offerings of a smaller  sequencing panel really takes us back to  what we were doing  about five or six years ago at  cedars-sinai and that we took  our 50 gene panel and we made we made  even  smaller sub-panels that were  more tailored to certain types of  tumors and so and and the second piece  is that the scheduling of the genexis  runs  will be flexible and flexibility as i  said earlier in the laboratory is key  because  of staffing is highly variable p your  staffing is not 24 7  and seven days a week and we have to be  able to be flexible and  to customize the days that the runs  are set up um having some having a  platform that  is fully optimal automated  will allow the technologists to have  minimal hands-on time  and that is a key component to the  uh to the genexis system uh currently we  have to have a technology  technologist available uh throughout the  day  even though the hands on time may be  mult they have to be available  multiple times a day on multiple  sequential days to get the  sequencing run completed  of having a flexible batch size is  something that also was very  interesting for us and that we would be  able to  run smaller number of samples and mix  the type of samples that we run  over a week or two week period allowing  us  to adjust to the incoming sample  volumes so overall we think that the  genexis system would be very useful  in addition to genetic profiling within  the  molecular pathology lab at cedar sinai  thank you are there any questions  thank you kevin for your time and  presentation today it was a very  insightful perspective and important  considerations  when deciding to bring ngs testing into  your lab and we do have a few questions  from the audience  question number one you mentioned that  your lab volume quadrupled with the  addition of sars kobe 2 testing  how will the genexis workflow fit in  with your lab and help your current  staffing needs  um so we believe that the genexis  platform will decrease the technologies  time significantly allowing us to  perform  additional testing including coded  testing  the tech time saved you know really is  from you know preparing the instrument  and reagents  from both the um from library  to templating and from templating to  sequencing  there is of the possibility of saving  that tech time  and freeing them up to not have to  perform testing  you know at specific times during the  day so this  will have a positive impact to the lab  great thank you question number two  besides being able to interrogate more  genes at a time with ngs testing over  single gene testing  reducing the cost per gene what were  other financial considerations in  deciding to bring  in the genexis um  i believe the the versatility of batch  size is probably the key  um key thing for the laboratory  um our current sequencing workflow  requires a set number  of samples per chip and as  if those if you do not reach that set  number of samples the price  significantly increases um the genexis  you know with the gx5 chip and the four  lanes will allow us  a little bit more flexibility um  having smaller batch sizes will also um  also allow us to  manage our turnaround time a little bit  more effectively  great thank you and thanks again for  your time and presentation today  that was definitely helpful information  if we did not get your question it will  be saved and we will get back to you  individually with response and the  presentation will be available on demand  with that this concludes our on combined  education day changing the precision  oncology research paradigm  i would like to thank dr eric bale dr  andy felton  and mr kevin kawachi for sharing their  valuable presentations with us today  we would like to thank you all in  attendance for your participation in  today's program  and remind you to please complete the  conclusion survey thanks again and enjoy  the rest of your week
 

